window.docContents[114].resolve({"/Hyperbolic-Equations/Propagation___-shocks___-and-weak-selection/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Propagation___-shocks___-and-weak-selection--Hypotheses-carried-by-each-solution-concept":{"contents":"Classical transport and wave statements carry differentiability, support,\nand initial-data hypotheses. Local energy is integrated over moving spatial\nregions, so finite propagation depends on the geometry of those regions as\nmuch as on the PDE identity.\n\nNonlinear first-order equations use characteristic ODEs for the position, the\nunknown, and the gradient, and a noncharacteristic condition is what makes the\ncharacteristic coordinates locally invertible. Conservation laws work\ndifferently, with distributional identities, entropy pairs, Rankine–Hugoniot\nrelations, and strong local initial traces.\n\nWeak wave equations live in a Gelfand triple with second-order time\ndynamics. Symmetric first-order systems carry matrix symmetry, coercivity,\nand coefficient regularity. Hamilton–Jacobi statements keep the classical,\nvariational, and viscosity notions apart rather than treating them as\nsynonyms.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Propagation, shocks, and weak selection","header":"7.1.2. Hypotheses carried by each solution concept","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/Propagation___-shocks___-and-weak-selection/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Propagation___-shocks___-and-weak-selection--Hypotheses-carried-by-each-solution-concept"},"/Parabolic-Equations/Finite-Time-Behavior/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Finite-Time-Behavior--Finite-Time-Growth___-Lifespan___-and-Similarity":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Finite-Time Behavior","header":"6.7.1. Finite-Time Growth, Lifespan, and Similarity","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/Finite-Time-Behavior/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Finite-Time-Behavior--Finite-Time-Growth___-Lifespan___-and-Similarity"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Sobolev-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--One-dimensional-and-coordinate-line-calculus":{"contents":"Every global  class has a selected representative that:\n\n* is absolutely continuous on every compact interval;* has classical derivative equal almost everywhere to the stored weak\nderivative;* satisfies the literal fundamental theorem of calculus.\n\nAbsolute value, positive and negative parts, and interval truncation have the\nexpected almost-everywhere derivative formulas for this representative.  They\nare also completed operations on , so later\nproofs need not rebuild graph-space elements from raw representatives.\n\nIn higher dimensions, coordinate insertion and Fubini give absolutely\ncontinuous representatives on almost every coordinate line.  The classical\nline derivative agrees almost everywhere with the corresponding weak partial\nderivative.  A two-way coordinate ACL characterization is available in the\nfinite-exponent setting used by the nonsmooth chain rule.\n\nThe line theorem is coordinatewise: absolute continuity holds on almost every\nline parallel to a coordinate axis.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Sobolev Coverage and Open Problems","header":"4.6.1.9. One-dimensional and coordinate-line calculus","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Sobolev-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--One-dimensional-and-coordinate-line-calculus"}});